WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.890 --> 00:00:03.660
Meet Bloomberg's own, David Nicholson.

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:08.040
Now his happy place is out in
the field on the open road.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.740
But like the rest of the world lately,

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:13.150
he's been working from home
a lot more than he's used to.

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:16.200
COVID-19 has prompted
a massive urban exodus,

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.900
as professionals of every ilk,

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.670
yearn for more space to live and work.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:23.350
As a result, rural areas have exploded,

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:25.640
and they're poised to
experience a resurgence

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:27.390
as more and more families pack up

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:31.430
and leave the big city to
try out life in the burbs.

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:33.670
But there's one problem with that.

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:36.343
Not all internet speeds are created equal.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:39.350
Anyone who might need to upload

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:41.530
or download large files for a living,

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:42.810
may not wanna pack up and move

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.683
to that cabin in the woods just yet.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:49.010
Now David's house isn't a
cabin, but it is in the woods,

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:52.130
and inexplicably, his
internet is workable.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:54.110
My internet is okay.

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:55.740
I pay a lot for it.

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:58.280
It's around $140 a month.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.090
The download speed's great,

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.620
and the upload speed is terrible,

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:04.300
which is really bad for my work

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:06.400
'cause I have to upload
these massive files.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.430
But I'm definitely one
of the more lucky ones

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:09.600
in this community.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:10.920
Just a few miles down the road,

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.210
my buddy has awful internet.

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:17.830
It's usually under two
down and under 0.2 up,

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:20.260
so it's often unusable.

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:23.600
Some days it's around one

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.943
and other times it'll
drop down to about .2, .3.

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:32.170
I just wanna be able to
have internet at my house

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:33.710
without having to go outside

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:37.686
the normal ways of acquiring internet,

00:01:37.686 --> 00:01:39.830
it's really frustrating.

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:43.780
We're literally miles, a few miles away,

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:46.730
from downtown, and it just cuts off.

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:50.320
This is a problem and the
solutions are not there yet

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.780
because getting high-speed
internet in rural areas en masse

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:57.473
is something our providers
may simply not be ready for.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:05.690
If your internet service doesn't have

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:07.860
fast download and upload speeds,

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:09.890
then you don't have high-speed Harmony.

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:11.290
You have .

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:16.090
Americans on average pay about $70 a month

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:17.340
for internet at home,

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.170
but in rural areas it
could cost a lot more

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:21.850
for a lot less service.

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:25.350
So the pricing for a fixed
wireless provider here

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.646
can be something between 80 and $150

00:02:27.646 --> 00:02:30.230
for about 25 megabits per second.

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:32.010
However, gigabit service,

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:34.850
which is a thousand megabits
per second, and symmetrical,

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:38.320
is running at about 119
a month or 130 a month.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:39.370
That's John Paul,

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:41.430
he co-owns Spiral Fiber,

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:42.930
an internet service provider here

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:44.710
in Nevada County, California.

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:46.540
And for the past 10
years, he's been trying to

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:49.970
bring an affordable fiber
optic network to town.

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:51.620
It all started when he applied to be part

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:54.610
of the pilot program for
Google's fiber service.

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:56.780
Across the country
civic boosters have been

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:59.277
going to extremes chanting ,

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.660
singing ♪ Google in the morning

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:08.190
and marching trying to get
the search engine giant

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:09.770
to look at their town.

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:13.570
The internet these days is
crucial, to be in the world,

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:15.060
to be a part of the world

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:17.160
and to be connected to your community.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:19.670
Google ultimately went
with Kansas city, Missouri

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:21.850
which left Paul on a
mission to get funding

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:25.230
to lay out fiber optic
networks for gigabit service.

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:28.160
Over the next several years
he would attend conferences

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:30.100
and pitch venture capitalists

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:33.433
and apply for grants to make
his vision come to life.

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:36.280
One of the biggest roadblocks

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:38.470
to building infrastructure
for the internet here

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:42.000
in the United States are the
existing large providers.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.470
They have their land staked out.

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:46.110
They would prefer that nobody come in

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:47.440
and compete against them.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:50.010
In 2013, we applied for a grant.

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:51.740
We went down and met
with the commissioners

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:53.610
at the California public
utilities commission.

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:56.320
It was an arduous process to get funded

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:58.460
but we finally did against all odds.

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:00.120
We got a $16 million grant

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:03.040
and then we went out to
seek venture capital.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:07.310
Then in March of 2020, the
pandemic hit the world.

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:10.380
Everybody got locked down
and everybody came home

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:12.380
and everybody started using the internet.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:13.720
And I remember seeing on one

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:16.230
of the local fixed wireless
providers, websites

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:18.837
they've said, "Hey, we know
you're all staying home

00:04:18.837 --> 00:04:20.757
"but please don't use too much video

00:04:20.757 --> 00:04:22.810
"and please don't use too much bandwidth."

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:25.950
And I thought "That doesn't work."

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:28.690
The U S connectivity gap
is far greater compared

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:31.420
to countries that are considered
its peers economically.

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:33.750
Some developing nations
actually have better internet

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:35.130
than rural America.

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:37.820
Like in Kenya which has a
state-of-the-art fiber optic

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:40.170
network fed in from their coastline.

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:42.330
The American connectivity gap does largely

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:45.130
affect rural areas that are either outside

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:48.613
of service coverage or simply
prohibitively expensive.

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:50.720
This is Rebecca.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:52.830
She and her family moved to Nevada County

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.660
just a couple miles down
the road from David.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:57.440
They took for granted
that high speed internet

00:04:57.440 --> 00:04:59.110
would just exist in the area.

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:01.140
And it's not something
they even bothered to check

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:03.230
on before buying their house.

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:06.040
Turns out, all they got was dial up.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:08.136
We wanted to move where we
have a little more space,

00:05:08.136 --> 00:05:11.170
room for the kids to run
around, you know, just be out

00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:12.500
in the mountains a little bit more.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:15.550
Having come from a bigger
city area where wifi is pretty

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:17.770
readily available, we
hadn't really considered

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:20.240
that in our purchasing of the house,

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:22.160
whether that would be an option or not.

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:25.050
But when school started and
now that we are remote learning

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:28.120
with our children and we're
doing everything from home,

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:30.030
we didn't have the option
to do that at our house.

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:33.020
So every day when school
would start, we'd have to

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:36.920
pack the kids up and we'd
go to my in-laws home.

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:38.440
They have a little cabin up in this area

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:40.020
that does have wifi already.

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:43.520
Rebecca's situation is
the norm in rural America.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:45.550
According to data from
the federal communications

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:49.970
commission, just 4% of
urban Americans lack access

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:51.920
to broadband internet.

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:54.847
That's compared to almost
40% of people who live

00:05:54.847 --> 00:05:56.650
in rural areas.

00:05:56.650 --> 00:05:59.430
But while that the case,
there were also racial

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:03.210
and socioeconomic divides in
terms of who had that access.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:05.100
In addition to that geographic difference

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:07.900
low-income African-American
students, Latino students

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:11.890
and first-generation college
students were more likely

00:06:11.890 --> 00:06:15.010
than others to have only one
device at home that shared

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:16.600
among multiple siblings

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:20.143
and or lack access to
that actual connection.

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:23.350
For most American towns
there's also a lack

00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:26.510
of competition, forcing internet prices up

00:06:26.510 --> 00:06:28.350
and large providers like AT&amp;T

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:31.330
and Comcast aren't
incentivized to expand service

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:32.680
to these rural communities.

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:37.000
This is how Xfinity makes
life simple, easy, awesome.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:40.310
So in our area, we have a
small imprint of Comcast.

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:42.500
But they're not gonna go
out to the more rural areas.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:43.950
It's just not cost-effective

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:46.030
for them to do it as a large company.

00:06:46.030 --> 00:06:48.900
In order to lay fiber cable,
you're actually physically

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:50.630
digging trenches into the ground.

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:53.780
And for internet service
providers, they're primarily

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:56.140
focused on maximizing their profits.

00:06:56.140 --> 00:06:58.920
And so the fewer customers
you have, you know

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:02.040
in any square mile and the
more fiber you have to lay

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:03.690
that's just kind of harder to pencil

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:06.564
out from a purely economic standpoint.

00:07:06.564 --> 00:07:08.480
AT&amp;T released a statement on the matter

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:09.313
expressing the need and desire
for government assistance

00:07:09.313 --> 00:07:14.264
to fund fiber optics expansion
to areas deemed unprofitable.

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:17.660
Comcast released a similar statement

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:19.970
encouraging more public
private collaboration

00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:21.480
on this effort.

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:24.000
Federal agencies do offer
grants to help communities

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.620
build broadband networks, but
many have argued that there's

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:29.880
not enough flexibility on
how to use these funds.

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:33.090
The COVID-19 relief package
passed by Congress in March

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:36.997
and signed by president Donald
Trump awarded $43.3 million

00:07:36.997 --> 00:07:40.860
to 51 projects on the East coast alone.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:43.810
But it also stipulated that
these projects needed to be up

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:45.493
and running by December 30th.

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:48.670
For contractors who plan projects, months

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:50.960
and years in advance it's a scramble

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:53.920
to rearrange their schedules
to meet that deadline.

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:55.782
So what we've been asking
the federal government

00:07:55.782 --> 00:07:58.790
to consider is at least as long

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:00.470
as the projects are getting started,

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:01.303
knowing the projects
are being put into place

00:08:01.303 --> 00:08:05.810
to expand both remote
learning, working from home

00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:07.260
and opportunities like that

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:08.900
that are truly driven from COVID.

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:10.540
We're moving as fast as we can regardless,

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:12.470
but hopefully they'll
give us some more time.

00:08:12.470 --> 00:08:15.500
Talking to John Paul it's
clear that he believes

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:18.450
broadband is something that
can transform a community

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:20.703
and should be considered a public good.

00:08:21.950 --> 00:08:23.740
One of the first communities to come on

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:25.740
in the United States with full-on gigabit

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:28.130
service was Chattanooga, Tennessee.

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:29.140
Now they're a bigger city

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:32.343
but they're a city that was
economically in a downturn.

00:08:33.310 --> 00:08:36.690
In the last 15 years the city
of Chattanooga has turned

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:39.190
around in an amazing way.

00:08:39.190 --> 00:08:41.550
It's a nexus for young people to move to.

00:08:41.550 --> 00:08:43.450
It's a place where technology happens.

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:47.760
It's not just about logging
on to Zoom, you know,

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:50.180
to connect with your colleagues who might

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:51.900
be in New York City.

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:55.773
It's also your ability as
a small business operating

00:08:55.773 --> 00:09:00.170
in the town of Nevada
City or wherever it may be

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:04.870
to quickly upload your payroll documents

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:08.420
or something onto the cloud,
or to quickly, you know,

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:10.670
swipe a customer's credit card.

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:13.670
There's just some really
basic business functions

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:15.460
and mechanics that I think people

00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:17.660
in big cities really take for granted.

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:19.610
And that are just gonna
be a much bigger challenge

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:22.663
for small business owners in rural areas.

